{
  "state": {
    "case_id": "356",
    "case_summary": "A 28-year-old white man presented with skin-colored, dome-shaped firm papules over the chin, nose, and perinasal region. The facial papules had increased in size and number since he first noticed them at about age 18 years. Medical history includes requiring a right renal transplant at age 14 years.\n\nPhysical examination revealed dozens of skin-colored dome-shaped papules over the chin, nasal, and perinasal regions of the face. No hypopigmented macules, periungual fibromas, or Shagreen plaques were present. Dental examination showed several teeth with a conical shape.\n\n- Histologic Analysis:    First biopsy of facial papule showed a dome-shaped lesion with several plump fibroblasts and increased blood vessels, compatible with an angiofibroma. No crystals were observed even with polarized light microscopy.        - Electron Microscopy:    Image Title: Biopsy Specimen From a Facial Papule Under Electron Microscopy    Image Description: Electron micrograph shows a dermal fibroblast with prominent crystalline inclusions within the cytoplasm and some smaller inclusions forming within lysosomes (magnification ×8000).",
